WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.302 --> 00:00:05.571 line:0%
This is me in China
in 1996,

00:00:05.604 --> 00:00:08.741 line:0%
on a trip to see
where my family came from.

00:00:08.774 --> 00:00:11.778
<i> That trip was the first time
 that I rode in an airplane,</i>

00:00:11.811 --> 00:00:14.380 line:0%
<i> and the first time
that I got stuck in an airport.</i>

00:00:14.413 --> 00:00:17.350
We've been waiting
here for 9 hours now!

00:00:17.383 --> 00:00:20.553
Air travel is
one of the great privileges
of living in this century.

00:00:20.586 --> 00:00:22.388
And the number
of air passengers

00:00:22.421 --> 00:00:25.224
is expected to double
in the next 20 years.

00:00:25.257 --> 00:00:27.427
There's just one problem.

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:30.496
Aviation runs on oil,
which contributes to global
climate change.

00:00:30.529 --> 00:00:32.599
<i> And there's not
 a good alternative yet.</i>

00:00:34.834 --> 00:00:36.702
<i> So individual consumers
 were left wondering</i>

00:00:36.735 --> 00:00:38.538
<i> about their own
 responsibility.</i>

00:00:38.571 --> 00:00:42.241
These blankets represent
arctic sea ice.

00:00:42.274 --> 00:00:44.777
Alex:
<i> How many polar bears
 did Cleo kill?</i>

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:49.315
<i> Shame arises when our values
don't line up with our actions.</i>

00:00:49.348 --> 00:00:51.384 size:88% position:44%
Oh, this is so embarrassing.

00:00:51.417 --> 00:00:54.153
Joss:
<i> So, knowing what we know
 about the climate crisis,</i>

00:00:54.186 --> 00:00:56.823
<i> is it wrong to fly?</i>

00:00:58.624 --> 00:01:01.394
<i> ( theme music playing )</i>

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:19.212 align:start position:0%
Okay, I want you guys
to think back on the past
year of your lives

00:01:19.245 --> 00:01:21.147 align:start position:0%
and remember all
of the flights you took,

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:23.216 align:start size:72% position:28% line:0%
both for work
and for personal trips.

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:25.284 align:start position:0% line:0%
I have a marker
for each of you.

00:01:25.317 --> 00:01:28.588 align:start position:0%
I want you to draw all
of those flights on these maps.

00:01:28.621 --> 00:01:30.523 align:start size:81% position:19%
- ( Alex chuckles )
- Amazing.

00:01:30.556 --> 00:01:32.792 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Let's see.
- A work trip for a story.

00:01:32.825 --> 00:01:37.130 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Cleo: DC to Detroit.
- New York to New Orleans.

00:01:39.465 --> 00:01:43.503 align:start position:0%
So, this flight right here
is a trip that I took
to Ireland this year,

00:01:43.536 --> 00:01:46.873 align:start position:0%
and on the way back,
we flew over the southern
tip of Greenland.

00:01:46.906 --> 00:01:48.474 size:81% position:41%
And I had the window seat.

00:01:48.507 --> 00:01:51.544 align:start position:0%
And when I looked
out the window,

00:01:51.577 --> 00:01:53.779 align:start position:0%
this is what I saw.

00:01:53.812 --> 00:01:55.681 size:88% position:44%
That is a melting ice sheet.

00:01:55.714 --> 00:01:58.151 align:start position:0%
<i>It's probably something
I would never see</i>

00:01:58.184 --> 00:02:00.186 align:start position:0%
<i>except for
in a plane like this.</i>

00:02:00.219 --> 00:02:02.388 align:start position:0%
But the plane
that I was flying in
is one of the reasons

00:02:02.421 --> 00:02:04.357 align:start position:0%
that that ice was melting
in the first place.

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:06.592 align:start position:0%
<i>Because air travel
accounts for about 5%</i>

00:02:06.625 --> 00:02:08.895 align:start position:0%
<i>of man-made global warming
in a year.</i>

00:02:08.928 --> 00:02:11.497 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Cleo:<i> Whoa. </i>
- Joss:<i> And that impact
 will only increase</i>

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:13.933
<i> as more and more people
 in Asia to take to the skies.</i>

00:02:13.966 --> 00:02:17.370 align:start size:88% position:13%
Okay, well, I have
all of your flight data.

00:02:17.403 --> 00:02:18.938
- Alex: Yeah.
- Cleo: Uh-oh.

00:02:18.971 --> 00:02:21.274 align:start size:81% position:19%
I'm going to be running
some calculations on this,

00:02:21.307 --> 00:02:23.543 align:start size:72% position:28%
and I will be bothering
you all very soon.

00:02:23.576 --> 00:02:25.411 align:start size:69% position:31%
- Okay.
- All right.

00:02:25.444 --> 00:02:26.812 align:start size:78% position:22%
- Okay? All right.
- All right.

00:02:26.845 --> 00:02:28.815
<i> ( music playing )</i>

00:02:33.786 --> 00:02:35.621
Everyone flies
for different reasons.

00:02:35.654 --> 00:02:38.691
The four of us,
we took 84 flights
in the past year,

00:02:38.724 --> 00:02:40.726
and almost half of those
were for work.

00:02:40.759 --> 00:02:43.362
The best way to find out
what impact that has
on the climate

00:02:43.395 --> 00:02:44.931
is to use
an online calculator

00:02:44.964 --> 00:02:47.800
that measures
the carbon footprint
of each flight.

00:02:47.833 --> 00:02:50.670 line:0%
<i> ( music playing )</i>

00:02:50.703 --> 00:02:53.306
<i> They ask about
 your connections
 because nonstop flights</i>

00:02:53.339 --> 00:02:55.308
<i> typically use fuel
 more efficiently.</i>

00:02:55.341 --> 00:02:56.943
<i> Something like
 a quarter of emissions</i>

00:02:56.976 --> 00:02:58.744
<i> are from take off
 and landing alone,</i>

00:02:58.777 --> 00:02:59.879
so you don't want
to do that twice

00:02:59.912 --> 00:03:01.414
if you don't have to.

00:03:01.447 --> 00:03:03.316
They also ask
whether you flew economy,

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:04.850
<i> business class,
 or first class,</i>

00:03:04.883 --> 00:03:06.986
because if you're taking up
more space on the plane,

00:03:07.019 --> 00:03:09.388
you're responsible
for more of the fuel use.

00:03:09.421 --> 00:03:10.923
The folks in first class

00:03:10.956 --> 00:03:13.292
<i> are causing about five times
 the emissions</i>

00:03:13.325 --> 00:03:14.594
<i> of the economy passengers.</i>

00:03:14.627 --> 00:03:15.962
Tsk, tsk, tsk.

00:03:15.995 --> 00:03:18.364
So, all of our flights
led up to the equivalent

00:03:18.397 --> 00:03:22.301
of 36.7 metric tons
of CO2.

00:03:22.334 --> 00:03:23.569
It's kinda hard
to know what that means

00:03:23.602 --> 00:03:25.538
because CO2 is invisible.

00:03:25.571 --> 00:03:27.907
<i> But there was a report
 published that shows us</i>

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:30.810
<i> how we can translate
 our CO2 emissions</i>

00:03:30.843 --> 00:03:35.014
<i> into actual square meters
 of melted arctic sea ice.</i>

00:03:35.047 --> 00:03:40.319 line:0%
What we find, I mean,
that basically the observed
sea ice loss

00:03:40.352 --> 00:03:42.021 line:0%
is very linearly related

00:03:42.054 --> 00:03:44.790
to how much CO2 we keep
adding to the atmosphere,

00:03:44.823 --> 00:03:48.828
such that for about, um,
every metric ton of CO2

00:03:48.861 --> 00:03:50.463
we add to the atmosphere,

00:03:50.496 --> 00:03:52.798
we melt another three square
meters of sea ice.

00:03:52.831 --> 00:03:56.369
<i> You know, whenever I fly
 from London to New York City,
 for example,</i>

00:03:56.402 --> 00:03:58.638
<i> that's equivalent to about
 a metric ton of CO2.</i>

00:03:58.671 --> 00:04:02.441
<i> So then I would melt
 about three square meters
 every time I flew.</i>

00:04:02.474 --> 00:04:05.378
<i> So, basically,
 the size of a large
 dinner table, I guess.</i>

00:04:15.688 --> 00:04:17.857 align:start size:41% position:59%
Where's Joss?

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:19.292
Here's Alex's.

00:04:19.325 --> 00:04:20.293
Where are we going?

00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:21.627 align:start size:47% position:53%
I have no idea.

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:22.895 size:56% position:72%
Do you guys know
where we're going?

00:04:22.928 --> 00:04:24.397 align:start size:97% position:3%
- No.
- I have a pretty good guess.

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:26.499 align:start size:69% position:31%
I heard a lot of talk
about blankets.

00:04:26.532 --> 00:04:29.702 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.
- Like picnic blankets?

00:04:29.735 --> 00:04:32.438 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Mm.
- Is there food there,
  is what I'm wondering.

00:04:32.471 --> 00:04:33.673 align:start size:59% position:41%
Christophe:
I don't think there
is gonna be food.

00:04:33.706 --> 00:04:35.341
No?

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:49.622 align:start size:66% position:34%
Welcome to the park.

00:04:49.655 --> 00:04:51.557 align:start size:59% position:41%
How long
have you been here?

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:52.725
I spent the night here.

00:04:52.758 --> 00:04:54.327
- Oh, nice.
- Oh, no.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:55.661 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Not really.
- At least you have blankets.

00:04:55.694 --> 00:04:57.029 size:78% position:39%
Yeah.

00:04:57.062 --> 00:04:58.764 size:84% position:58%
You ready to have some fun?

00:04:58.797 --> 00:05:00.433 align:start size:97% position:3%
Uh, y-- I think so.

00:05:00.466 --> 00:05:01.801 align:start size:69% position:31%
Okay, so, this morning

00:05:01.834 --> 00:05:03.936 align:start size:69% position:31%
we're gonna be using
our imagination.

00:05:03.969 --> 00:05:07.573 align:start size:69% position:31%
So, these blankets
are arctic sea ice.

00:05:07.606 --> 00:05:08.974 align:start size:66% position:34%
And of course
they're just blankets

00:05:09.007 --> 00:05:10.609 align:start size:88% position:13%
that we're gonna be donating
to Providence House

00:05:10.642 --> 00:05:12.011 align:start size:59% position:41%
here in Brooklyn
when we're done.

00:05:12.044 --> 00:05:13.713 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Nice.
- But for now they're sea ice.

00:05:13.746 --> 00:05:15.047 align:start size:75% position:25%
And arctic sea ice,
of course,

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:17.917 align:start size:88% position:13%
has been melting due
to man-made global warming.

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:20.753
So, we'll start over here.

00:05:20.786 --> 00:05:23.789 align:start position:0%
If you have meat in your diet
for a year in the U.S.,

00:05:23.822 --> 00:05:26.926 size:84% position:42%
you melt this much sea ice.

00:05:26.959 --> 00:05:29.662
Alex: Oh, my God.

00:05:29.695 --> 00:05:31.897 align:start position:0%
And that is actually
the square footage.

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:33.599 align:start size:75% position:25%
- Exactly, yes.
- Wow.

00:05:33.632 --> 00:05:36.469 align:start position:0%
If you drive a car
for a year in the U.S.,

00:05:36.502 --> 00:05:39.772 align:start position:0%
Alex, you melt
this much sea ice.

00:05:39.805 --> 00:05:40.873 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Should I roll this?
- Yeah.

00:05:40.906 --> 00:05:41.908 align:start size:34% position:66%
Okay.

00:05:43.642 --> 00:05:45.778 align:start position:0%
Oh, this is shameful.

00:05:45.811 --> 00:05:46.812 align:start size:47% position:53%
Whoa.

00:05:46.845 --> 00:05:48.714 align:start size:50% position:50%
What kind of car
do you drive?

00:05:48.747 --> 00:05:51.117 size:75% position:38%
I drive a Nissan Silvia.

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:53.018 align:start position:0%
Oh, that's devastating.

00:05:53.051 --> 00:05:54.720 align:start size:81% position:19%
You probably drive less
than the average American.

00:05:54.753 --> 00:05:56.422
Yeah, but it only gets,
like, 16 miles to the gallon.

00:05:56.455 --> 00:05:57.857 align:start size:91% position:9%
Not great.

00:05:57.890 --> 00:05:59.625 align:start size:59% position:41%
And this is the ice
that melts

00:05:59.658 --> 00:06:02.128 align:start size:84% position:16%
from the average American's
air travel in a year.

00:06:02.161 --> 00:06:03.896 align:start size:50% position:50%
And that doesn't
seem like a lot

00:06:03.929 --> 00:06:05.765 align:start size:84% position:16%
because it's the equivalent
of just one round trip

00:06:05.798 --> 00:06:07.500 align:start size:50% position:50%
between New York
and California.

00:06:07.533 --> 00:06:09.101 align:start size:56% position:44%
But that generates
the same emissions

00:06:09.134 --> 00:06:11.837 align:start size:66% position:34%
as around four months
of driving.

00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:13.706 align:start size:94% position:6%
But, of course,
the four of us,

00:06:13.739 --> 00:06:15.107 align:start size:94% position:6%
we are not
the average flyer.

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:16.542
Oh, no.

00:06:16.575 --> 00:06:17.943 align:start position:0%
So, we're gonna go
over there now.

00:06:17.976 --> 00:06:20.480 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Oof.
- Ours are gonna be huge.

00:06:22.948 --> 00:06:24.917 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Uh-oh.
- ( chuckles )

00:06:24.950 --> 00:06:28.488 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I think I see my pile.
- Yeah, same.

00:06:32.090 --> 00:06:33.859 align:start position:0%
Okay, we're gonna start
with this one.

00:06:33.892 --> 00:06:35.428 align:start position:0%
You guys want to help me
fold it out?

00:06:35.461 --> 00:06:37.630 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah.
- Let's just start with half.

00:06:37.663 --> 00:06:39.799 align:start size:66% position:34%
This way,
and then it goes...

00:06:47.005 --> 00:06:49.542 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Christophe: Oh, my God.
- Alex: Ooh, wow.

00:06:49.575 --> 00:06:50.843
I was gonna say it's like
the car, but this is--

00:06:50.876 --> 00:06:52.511 align:start size:84% position:16%
- This is yours?
- Joss: This is mine.

00:06:52.544 --> 00:06:53.546
- What?
- Whoa.

00:06:53.579 --> 00:06:55.581 align:start size:78% position:22%
Oh, no.

00:06:55.614 --> 00:06:59.185 size:84% position:42%
But this isn't even, like--
this is just you flying.

00:06:59.218 --> 00:07:02.955 align:start size:91% position:9%
- This isn't even you
  as an individual with-- 
- In a year. Right.

00:07:02.988 --> 00:07:05.658 align:start size:72% position:28%
Okay, now let's go over
and check this one out.

00:07:11.597 --> 00:07:13.098 align:start size:69% position:31%
- It's Alex.
- Alex!

00:07:13.131 --> 00:07:14.834 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah, that's me.
- Joss: You did pretty good.

00:07:14.867 --> 00:07:16.702 size:84% position:58%
Still more than the average
American, though, right?

00:07:16.735 --> 00:07:17.970 align:start position:0%
Still more than
the average American.

00:07:18.003 --> 00:07:20.105
Yeah, I'm like two Americans.

00:07:20.138 --> 00:07:22.074
Still not good.

00:07:22.107 --> 00:07:24.743 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Okay, big boy.
- Who could this be?

00:07:24.776 --> 00:07:26.612
Oh, no!

00:07:26.645 --> 00:07:28.180
Oh, this is so embarrassing.

00:07:28.213 --> 00:07:30.616 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Cleo: Whee!
- This just keeps going.

00:07:30.649 --> 00:07:32.952 align:start size:69% position:31%
Well, I feel terrible.

00:07:32.985 --> 00:07:35.922 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Okay, let's do the last one.
- ( Cleo groans )

00:07:37.856 --> 00:07:40.593 align:start size:94% position:6%
I like how the number of times
it has to be folded...

00:07:40.626 --> 00:07:41.627 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Tells you a lot. Right.
- ...kind of gives us
  an idea.

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:43.062
Cleo:
Oh, my God.

00:07:49.001 --> 00:07:50.536
Alex:
It's like a small whale.

00:07:50.569 --> 00:07:51.971
Christophe:
Oh, no.

00:07:52.004 --> 00:07:53.806
Cleo:
It's like a big whale.

00:07:53.839 --> 00:07:56.008 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Well, it's a lot of ice.
- It's a lot of ice.

00:07:57.776 --> 00:07:59.111 align:start size:78% position:22%
Joss: But it's not really
about any individual.

00:07:59.144 --> 00:08:00.779 align:start size:81% position:19%
I mean...

00:08:00.812 --> 00:08:03.215 align:start size:63% position:38%
Like, you flew
all over the place
for work this year.

00:08:03.248 --> 00:08:05.518 align:start size:81% position:19%
- It is mostly work.
- Yeah.

00:08:05.551 --> 00:08:08.721 align:start size:91% position:9%
- But still.
- Maybe something
  for Vox to think about.

00:08:08.754 --> 00:08:09.955
Christophe:
Yeah.

00:08:09.988 --> 00:08:13.159
<i> ( music playing )</i>

00:08:15.227 --> 00:08:18.264 align:start size:56% position:44%
Oh, it's so heavy.

00:08:18.297 --> 00:08:19.832 size:84% position:58%
Yeah.

00:08:19.865 --> 00:08:21.133
Oh, my bad.
( laughs )

00:08:21.166 --> 00:08:22.635
Joss:
<i> So, altogether, we flew</i>

00:08:22.668 --> 00:08:24.570
<i> on 84 airplanes
 in the past year.</i>

00:08:24.603 --> 00:08:27.806
- Alex:<i> 84 separate airplanes? </i>
- Joss:<i> 84 separate airplanes.</i>

00:08:27.839 --> 00:08:30.109
<i> Do you guys know
 how many flights there are</i>

00:08:30.142 --> 00:08:31.911
<i> in a year around the world?</i>

00:08:31.944 --> 00:08:33.679 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Oh, hundreds of thousands?
- In a year?

00:08:33.712 --> 00:08:36.982 size:88% position:56%
There are 35 million flights
around the world in a year.

00:08:37.015 --> 00:08:38.551 align:start size:41% position:59%
Christophe:
35 million.

00:08:38.584 --> 00:08:41.554 align:start size:72% position:28%
Which is almost 100,000
every single day.

00:08:41.587 --> 00:08:43.322 align:start size:22% position:78%
Huh.

00:08:43.355 --> 00:08:47.626 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So, let's go
  sit down over here.
- Alex: Yeah.

00:08:47.659 --> 00:08:49.094
Joss:
<i> And what's interesting
 about that</i>

00:08:49.127 --> 00:08:50.896
<i> is that all those flights
 are being taken</i>

00:08:50.929 --> 00:08:53.065
<i> by a minority
 of the world's population.</i>

00:08:53.098 --> 00:08:54.700 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Mm-hmm.
- So, by some estimates,

00:08:54.733 --> 00:08:57.703
only 20% of humans
have ever flown on an airplane.

00:08:57.736 --> 00:08:59.271 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Cleo: Whoa.
- Wow.

00:08:59.304 --> 00:09:01.674 align:start position:0%
And even within the U.S.,
only about half of Americans

00:09:01.707 --> 00:09:03.742 align:start position:0%
fly in a given year.

00:09:03.775 --> 00:09:06.912 align:start position:0%
It's about 12% of adults
in the U.S.

00:09:06.945 --> 00:09:10.849 align:start position:0%
that are taking
70% of the flights.
People like us.

00:09:10.882 --> 00:09:14.086
And that's where it gets
into the big ethical issue
with climate change,

00:09:14.119 --> 00:09:17.957
is that the people
who have used the most
fossil fuels

00:09:17.990 --> 00:09:19.992
generally have more resources

00:09:20.025 --> 00:09:22.227
to deal with the impacts
of climate change.

00:09:22.260 --> 00:09:24.730
So we're talking
about stronger storms,

00:09:24.763 --> 00:09:28.000
more floods, more droughts,
deadly heat waves.

00:09:28.033 --> 00:09:30.235
It's really the world's poor

00:09:30.268 --> 00:09:31.971
who are the most vulnerable,

00:09:32.004 --> 00:09:34.073
and in most cases,
we're talking about people

00:09:34.106 --> 00:09:37.676
who have never seen
the inside of an airplane.

00:09:37.709 --> 00:09:39.945
<i> And some people
 are taking this so seriously</i>

00:09:39.978 --> 00:09:42.214
<i> that they're completely
 changing the way they travel.</i>

00:09:46.251 --> 00:09:47.953 align:start size:59% position:41%
Is this 16-year-old
young lady

00:09:47.986 --> 00:09:50.389 align:start size:88% position:13%
now the leader of
the climate change movement?

00:09:50.422 --> 00:09:52.024 align:start position:0%
 Anchor #2:
<i>She is definitely the face.</i>

00:09:52.057 --> 00:09:53.826 align:start size:84% position:16%
 Anchor:
<i> And she's given a big push</i>

00:09:53.859 --> 00:09:55.861 align:start size:66% position:34%
to the flight shaming
movement.

00:09:55.894 --> 00:09:59.932 align:start size:81% position:19%
That is you're called out,
you're shamed if you fly.

00:09:59.965 --> 00:10:04.870
So, Joss asked me
to go see Greta Thunberg
arrive in New York City.

00:10:04.903 --> 00:10:07.706
<i> She's been sailing
 across the Atlantic</i>

00:10:07.739 --> 00:10:10.275
<i> for the last two weeks,
 I think.</i>

00:10:10.308 --> 00:10:13.846
<i> Her trip is a part
 of a larger movement
 called</i> "flygskam,"

00:10:13.879 --> 00:10:15.848
which is Swedish
for "flying shame."

00:10:15.881 --> 00:10:18.384
<i> And, honestly,
 it's kind of working.</i>

00:10:18.417 --> 00:10:20.052
In Sweden
and other parts of Europe,

00:10:20.085 --> 00:10:22.121
<i> people are starting
 to brag on social media</i>

00:10:22.154 --> 00:10:24.857
<i> about traveling by train
 instead of by plane.</i>

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:27.059
<i> And the data shows
 that in Swedish airports,</i>

00:10:27.092 --> 00:10:28.694
<i> passenger counts
 are down as well.</i>

00:10:46.111 --> 00:10:47.179
Cleo:
You look great.

00:10:49.414 --> 00:10:52.685
That diagonal sail,
that's her.

00:10:52.718 --> 00:10:54.753
( crowd chanting )
Greta! Greta! Greta!

00:10:54.786 --> 00:10:57.823
And she's about to set foot
on land for the first time.

00:10:57.856 --> 00:11:00.192
( crowd cheering )

00:11:03.762 --> 00:11:06.799
Greta Thunberg:
Well, all of this
is very overwhelming,

00:11:06.832 --> 00:11:10.135
and the ground
is still shaking for me.

00:11:10.168 --> 00:11:12.738 align:start size:78% position:22%
- ( Skype rings )
- Hey.

00:11:12.771 --> 00:11:14.473 line:0%
- Hey, Joss.
- So, Umair.

00:11:14.506 --> 00:11:16.375
You've been following
the flight shame movement

00:11:16.408 --> 00:11:18.010
as it's been
developing this year.

00:11:18.043 --> 00:11:19.144
What are some
of the critiques

00:11:19.177 --> 00:11:20.946
of the shaming approach?

00:11:20.979 --> 00:11:22.448
Well, in order
to solve climate change,

00:11:22.481 --> 00:11:24.183
a massive global problem,

00:11:24.216 --> 00:11:26.251
you really need to be taking
big bites of the apple.

00:11:26.284 --> 00:11:28.420
And the critique here
is that focusing

00:11:28.453 --> 00:11:29.855
on what individuals do

00:11:29.888 --> 00:11:31.423
kind of deflects
the responsibility

00:11:31.456 --> 00:11:33.025
from the institutional actors

00:11:33.058 --> 00:11:35.160
that really need to be
making these changes.

00:11:35.193 --> 00:11:37.863
And when it comes to Greta
specifically,

00:11:37.896 --> 00:11:40.065
she's obviously very famous
for avoiding flying,

00:11:40.098 --> 00:11:42.234
but her message
is a lot broader than that,
isn't that right?

00:11:42.267 --> 00:11:47.740 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Oh, absolutely.
- My message is that
  we'll be watching you.

00:11:47.773 --> 00:11:52.377
The eyes of all future
generations are upon you.

00:11:52.410 --> 00:11:54.313
And if you choose to fail us,

00:11:54.346 --> 00:11:57.816
I say we will never
forgive you.

00:11:57.849 --> 00:12:01.053
She does shame grown-ups,
but not about their flying,

00:12:01.086 --> 00:12:02.488
<i> but about their inaction,</i>

00:12:02.521 --> 00:12:04.423
<i> and I think if you
 are gonna shame people,</i>

00:12:04.456 --> 00:12:06.025
that might be
the way to do it.

00:12:15.066 --> 00:12:16.068
So, this is awkward.

00:12:16.101 --> 00:12:17.336
In order to finish this story,

00:12:17.369 --> 00:12:19.972
we are taking a plane
to California--

00:12:20.005 --> 00:12:22.508
